# Viridis colour scales: scale_viridis

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_colour_viridis_d()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_viridis.html), which
covers `scale_fill_viridis_d()`, `scale_colour_viridis_c()`, `scale_fill_viridis_c()`,
`scale_colour_viridis_b()` and `scale_fill_viridis_b()` too, in the order the page gives them.
Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps
its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

The viridis scales colour with palettes from the viridisLite package, which change evenly to the
eye in colour and in black and white, and are designed to be read by people with common forms of
colour blindness. `scale_colour_viridis_d()` maps a discrete column, `scale_colour_viridis_c()` a
continuous one, and `scale_colour_viridis_b()` a continuous one cut into bins; `option` picks the
palette, viridis by default, and the `fill` versions do the same for fill. Giggly has none of this
yet: it has no aesthetic but `x`, `y`, `group` and `weight`, no legends and no scale functions.

## The default for ordered values

The example draws a random sample of the `diamonds` dataset, picked after `set.seed(596)`.
`clarity` is an ordered factor, so ggplot2 colours it with viridis without being asked.

```r
# viridis is the default colour/fill scale for ordered factors
set.seed(596)
dsamp <- diamonds[sample(nrow(diamonds), 1000), ]
ggplot(dsamp, aes(carat, price)) +
  geom_point(aes(colour = clarity))
```

Not in Giggly yet: the `diamonds` dataset, mapping `colour`, the scale that maps it and the
legend that reads it back.

## Discrete values

`txsamp` keeps the rows of the `txhousing` dataset for the cities named.

```r
# Use viridis_d with discrete data
txsamp <- subset(txhousing, city %in%
  c("Houston", "Fort Worth", "San Antonio", "Dallas", "Austin"))
(d <- ggplot(data = txsamp, aes(x = sales, y = median)) +
   geom_point(aes(colour = city)))
d + scale_colour_viridis_d()
```

Not in Giggly yet: the `txhousing` dataset, mapping `colour`, the scale that maps it,
`scale_colour_viridis_d()` and the legend that reads it back.

## The legend's title

```r
# Change scale label
d + scale_colour_viridis_d("City\nCenter")
```

Not in Giggly yet: the `txhousing` dataset, mapping `colour`, `scale_colour_viridis_d()`, the
legend that reads it back, and titles and labels.

## Other palettes

```r
# Select palette to use, see ?scales::pal_viridis for more details
d + scale_colour_viridis_d(option = "plasma")
d + scale_colour_viridis_d(option = "inferno")
```

Not in Giggly yet: the `txhousing` dataset, mapping `colour`, `scale_colour_viridis_d()` and its
`option`, and the legend that reads it back.

## Fill colours

```r
# scale_fill_viridis_d works just the same as
# scale_colour_viridis_d but for fill colours
p <- ggplot(txsamp, aes(x = median, fill = city)) +
  geom_histogram(position = "dodge", binwidth = 15000)
p + scale_fill_viridis_d()
# the order of colour can be reversed
p + scale_fill_viridis_d(direction = -1)
```

Not in Giggly yet: the `txhousing` dataset, histograms (`geom_histogram()`) and the binning they
count with (`stat_bin()`), mapping `fill`, `scale_fill_viridis_d()` and its `direction` option,
and the legend that reads it back.

## Continuous values

```r
# Use viridis_c with continuous data
(v <- ggplot(faithfuld) +
  geom_tile(aes(waiting, eruptions, fill = density)))
v + scale_fill_viridis_c()
v + scale_fill_viridis_c(option = "plasma")
```

Not in Giggly yet: the `faithfuld` dataset, tiles (`geom_tile()`), mapping `fill`, the scale that
maps it, `scale_fill_viridis_c()` and its `option`, and the legend that reads it back.

## Binned values

```r
# Use viridis_b to bin continuous data before mapping
v + scale_fill_viridis_b()
```

Not in Giggly yet: the `faithfuld` dataset, `geom_tile()`, mapping `fill`,
`scale_fill_viridis_b()` and the legend that reads it back.
